WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.808
A typical IoT device is a system of
systems.

2
00:00:02.888 --> 00:00:05.708
It may include a bootloader, application
firmware,

3
00:00:05.788 --> 00:00:09.827
an RTOS or bare-metal runtime, drivers,
interrupts, sensors, actuators,

4
00:00:09.907 --> 00:00:13.561
local radios, wired interfaces, storage,
configuration records,

5
00:00:13.641 --> 00:00:17.872
identity material, mobile application
workflows, and cloud communication.

6
00:00:17.952 --> 00:00:21.670
Security review starts by understanding
how those pieces interact.

7
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:25.019
Bootloaders and startup code establish
early assumptions.

8
00:00:25.099 --> 00:00:27.984
Application firmware implements product
behavior.

9
00:00:28.064 --> 00:00:31.141
Drivers translate hardware status into
software state.

10
00:00:31.221 --> 00:00:34.811
Sensors and actuators connect the software
to the physical world.

11
00:00:34.891 --> 00:00:38.801
Communication components connect the
device to local users, gateways,

12
00:00:38.881 --> 00:00:40.676
mobile apps, and cloud services.

13
00:00:40.756 --> 00:00:43.064
Each area has a different authority level.

14
00:00:43.144 --> 00:00:45.708
A trust boundary is a point where data,
control,

15
00:00:45.788 --> 00:00:48.737
or authority crosses between different
assumptions.

16
00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:50.997
Data may move from a sensor into a driver,

17
00:00:51.077 --> 00:00:53.320
from a local radio into command handling,

18
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.708
from a cloud response into configuration,

19
00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:58.032
from stored state into startup behavior,

20
00:00:58.112 --> 00:01:00.484
or from a factory tool into device
identity.

21
00:01:00.564 --> 00:01:03.512
These crossings need validation and clear
ownership.

22
00:01:03.592 --> 00:01:07.310
Stored configuration can change device
behavior as much as code can.

23
00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:11.429
Feature flags, calibration records,
endpoint addresses, certificates,

24
00:01:11.509 --> 00:01:13.176
keys, counters, update state,

25
00:01:13.256 --> 00:01:17.038
and recovery markers should be treated as
security-relevant inputs.

26
00:01:17.118 --> 00:01:20.772
Reviewers should ask where each value
comes from, who may change it,

27
00:01:20.852 --> 00:01:22.711
and how it is validated before use.

28
00:01:22.791 --> 00:01:25.355
Device identity anchors many later
decisions.

29
00:01:25.435 --> 00:01:28.705
If the device uses a certificate, key,
token, serial number,

30
00:01:28.785 --> 00:01:32.951
or enrolled identity, the architecture
should show where it is provisioned,

31
00:01:33.031 --> 00:01:35.403
where it is stored, which code may access
it,

32
00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:37.919
and how it is used by local or remote
services.

33
00:01:37.999 --> 00:01:41.140
Identity is not just a string; it is part
of the trust model.

34
00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.208
Boundary mapping improves testing.

35
00:01:43.288 --> 00:01:47.326
Once interfaces, stored state, privileged
paths, and owners are visible,

36
00:01:47.406 --> 00:01:51.317
teams can design tests for malformed
messages, missing configuration,

37
00:01:51.397 --> 00:01:54.858
reset timing, failed credentials, expired
identity material,

38
00:01:54.938 --> 00:01:58.143
unreachable services, and production build
differences.

39
00:01:58.223 --> 00:02:00.980
A map turns security review into
engineering work.
